.TH LORIGINTEST 1
.SH NAME
lorigintest \- check that moving a window leaves nothing behind
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lorigintest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Lorigintest
moves a window and checks two things about the result: that none of the
window is left at the place it came from, and that whatever was underneath
it is revealed rather than painted over.
Both halves are checked because the obvious fix for the first causes the
second.
.PP
It runs entirely offscreen.
A
.B Screen
is allocated over an ordinary image rather than over the display's own
screen, so
.B readpixels
works \(em on the display's screen it reads back black \(em and nothing
here depends on a window system, on
.IR tk (2),
or on any particular host graphics backend.
.I Image.origin
reaches
.B memlorigin
through the same
.RB ` o '
message
.IR draw (3)
uses for a real window move, so the path under test is the real one.
.PP
Two arrangements are measured.
In the first a single window sits on nothing but the screen's fill, which
is the case where no layer lies behind to be exposed.
In the second a window overlaps one behind it, so part of what it vacates
belongs to another window and part to the background.
.PP
Every arrangement states its premises first \(em that the window really
did cover its own rectangle, that the one behind really did show where the
front one did not reach \(em so that a broken harness reads as a failure
rather than as a pass.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/lorigintest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR draw (2),
.IR draw (3),
.IR tkofftest (1)
.SH BUGS
.B Closing
a window still leaves an afterimage, and this does not catch it: the
scenario is in the source as
.I closed()
and is deliberately not called, because calling it turns the suite red.
Enabling it is a one-line change to
.IR init ().
.PP
The cause is the same missing case as the move had.
.I Memldelete
reaches it directly, with no shadow window and nothing to restore the
background, so a window dropped over bare background stays on the screen.
It is unfixed because the safe place to fix it cannot be established from
here:
.B f7ce10cad
removed the fill paint to close two races in which a dying layer's
footprint stomped another screen's correct draw \(em all screens share
one pixel buffer \(em and those races reached the painting step with
.I drawgoodname
already true, so neither that test nor a screen-liveness check excludes
them.  They are timing races between screens, and nothing single-threaded
and offscreen reproduces them; the suite that found them drives real
resizes and needs a window context this has none of.
.PP
There is now a check with teeth for that, and the thing it tests is
ORDER.  A teardown paint can only destroy something that is already
there, so the replacement content has to be drawn
.I before
the old screen goes away \(em which is the order
.IR wmclient (2)'s
.I putimage
uses, and the only order in which the race can bite.  Drawn afterwards it
would always win and would prove nothing; that was the first version of
this check, and it passed against a deliberately unguarded build, which
is how the mistake showed.
.PP
With the order right it is decisive.  An unguarded delete fails it, 3600
of 3600 pixels wrong \(em and so does a delete guarded on
.I drawgoodname
and on the screen outliving the window, which was the obvious fix and is
why that fix is not here.  Anything that proposes to restore the
background on close has to pass this.
.PP
Otherwise it covers the
.B libmemlayer
path and nothing downstream of it.
A window move also passes through the host's own upload path \(em on
MacOSX the tile marking in
.B win-cocoa.m
\(em and this never reaches it, so an afterimage that appeared only there
would not be caught here.
.PP
Colours are compared byte by byte, and
.B RGB24
.B readpixels
hands back blue, green, red in that order.
Assuming the other order inverts every check, and did.
